DTLS 1.3 only, TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384/AES-256-GCM-only, X25519MLKEM768
preferred by default, a working PQC emergency kill switch, and a fail-closed
post-handshake policy check (findings #8, #9, #10, #11).

Purpose. Strict (1:1) builds must negotiate DTLS 1.3 with AES-256-GCM only,
prefer the PQC hybrid group X25519MLKEM768 from the first ClientHello with no
field trial needed, and refuse (not silently accept) a handshake that somehow
still lands on something weaker. Switchable (LiveKit) builds keep upstream
behaviour by default and can only be tightened at runtime, one level at a
time, through Q-Audion's own API (rtc_base/qaudion_tuning.h, P8 - not written
by this patch). Every fact below about BoringSSL was checked against the
actual pinned sources, not just plan notes.

Per-file changes:

- rtc_base/openssl_stream_adapter.cc / .h (SetupSSLContext, ContinueSSL,
  Error, the constructor, the DTLS retransmission timer, new
  QaudionHandshakeMeetsPolicy()):

  * DTLS 1.3 as the floor. SetupSSLContext()'s min_version becomes
    DTLS1_3_VERSION once qaudion::TransportLevel() >= 3 (strict builds are
    always at level 3); TLS mode (OpenSSLAdapter's TURN-TLS, a different
    class) is untouched. A peer limited to DTLS 1.2 fails the handshake
    outright instead of negotiating down - the app then falls back to the
    sealed relay path (see webrtc-plan.md section 3.1), it does not retry
    ICE.
    Added a build-time guard, #ifndef DTLS1_3_VERSION #error ..., since the
    whole patch is unconditionally written in terms of that macro (even the
    switchable variant's fail-closed path needs to name DTLS 1.3
    explicitly). Verified present at this exact pin: BoringSSL f91f1447,
    include/openssl/ssl.h:553 (#define DTLS1_3_VERSION 0xfefc), and this
    file already branches on #ifdef DTLS1_3_VERSION for
    MAX_SSL_PROTOCOL_DTLS/GetMaxVersion() today, so the ifdef is
    unconditionally true in this codebase already - the #error just makes
    that assumption explicit and loud if it ever stops being true.

  * Finding #8 (HIGH): fixed the LiveKit cipher-list bug from the first
    review. BoringSSL's ssl_cipher.cc kCipherAliases table (checked at this
    pin) does NOT define "NULL", "aNULL", "SHA256", "SHA384" or "aECDH" as
    cipher-list rule words - only e.g. "aPSK", "3DES", "AESGCM", "AES256",
    "SHA1"/"SHA", "DEFAULT" survive from that style of list.
    SSL_CTX_set_cipher_list() "tolerates garbage inputs, unless an empty
    cipher list results" (ssl.h doc comment), so upstream's existing list -
    "DEFAULT:!NULL:!aNULL:!SHA256:!SHA384:!aECDH:!AESGCM+AES256:!aPSK:!3DES"
    - has always silently no-op'd those five unknown words; it was never
    meant to be run through the *strict* validator.
    SSL_CTX_set_strict_cipher_list() by contrast "returns error if str
    contains anything meaningless" - so passing the upstream list to it, as
    an earlier draft of this patch did, made SetupSSLContext() fail (and
    every DTLS handshake at levels 0-1 with it) before the connection even
    started. Fix: keep the upstream list on the tolerant
    SSL_CTX_set_cipher_list() unconditionally (it is exactly upstream's
    byte-for-byte behaviour at level < 2); use
    SSL_CTX_set_strict_cipher_list() only for our own hand-picked AES-256
    list, "ECDHE-ECDSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384:ECDHE-RSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384" - both
    names verified present in tls1.h (TLS1_TXT_ECDHE_ECDSA_WITH_AES_256_GCM_SHA384
    / TLS1_TXT_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_AES_256_GCM_SHA384) - at level >= 2 (strict
    builds: always). Also calls SSL_CTX_set_compliance_policy(ctx,
    ssl_compliance_policy_cnsa_202407) at that same level, which puts
    AES-256-GCM first in our ClientHello and makes us select it as server
    (BoringSSL ssl_lib.cc's cnsa_202407 Configure() step); this alone does
    NOT forbid AES-128/ChaCha20 on a TLS 1.3 connection (cipher lists do not
    apply to TLS 1.3 at all - "TLS 1.3 ciphers do not participate in this
    mechanism", ssl.h) or against a peer with its own stale cipher list -
    QaudionHandshakeMeetsPolicy() below is the actual fail-closed backstop.

  * Finding #10 (LOW), two parts:
    1. A new check in ContinueSSL()'s SSL_ERROR_NONE case, right after the
       existing RTC_DCHECK(peer_cert_chain_ || ...) and before state_ is
       ever set to SSL_CONNECTED or SE_OPEN is fired: if
       (ssl_mode_ == SSL_MODE_DTLS && !QaudionHandshakeMeetsPolicy()),
       report SSLHandshakeError::INCOMPATIBLE_CIPHERSUITE via
       handshake_error_ (same enum value the existing SSL_R_NO_SHARED_CIPHER
       branch below already uses), send a fatal alert 71
       (insufficient_security, SSL_AD_INSUFFICIENT_SECURITY - verified
       TLS1_AD_INSUFFICIENT_SECURITY == 71 in tls1.h) via
       Error("Q-AUDION dtls-policy", -1, SSL_AD_INSUFFICIENT_SECURITY,
       /*signal=*/true), and return -1 so the caller treats this exactly
       like the pre-existing handshake-failure branch (no ICE restart; the
       app falls back to the sealed relay path).
    2. The DTLS retransmission timer's lambda (SetTimeout()) used to call
       ContinueSSL() and drop its return value entirely - unlike BeginSSL()
       and OnEvent(), which both do
       `if (int error = ContinueSSL()) { Error(...); return; }`. A
       handshake or policy failure that happened to surface on exactly a
       retransmission timeout (not a socket read) never reached Error() at
       all, leaving the stream stuck in SSL_CONNECTING. Fixed to match the
       other two call sites.
    Because Error() can now legitimately be invoked twice for the same
    failure (once from inside ContinueSSL()'s new policy check, once again
    from whichever of the three call sites above also sees ContinueSSL()'s
    nonzero return and calls Error() itself), Error() itself gained a small
    idempotency guard: if state_ is already SSL_ERROR or SSL_CLOSED, return
    immediately. Cleanup() already tolerated re-entry (ssl_/ssl_ctx_ become
    null after the first call and are checked before use), but without this
    guard a second call would still re-fire SE_CLOSE and double-notify
    observers.

  * Finding #11 (LOW): the constructor's disable_ssl_group_ids_ no longer
    reads the "WebRTC-DisableSslGroupIds" field trial in strict builds - it
    is hard-wired false. That trial is upstream's kill switch for calling
    SSL_set1_group_ids() at all; left honored in a strict build it would
    fall the group preference back to BoringSSL's own default order, which
    neither includes X25519MLKEM768 nor excludes groups that don't pair with
    AES-256-GCM. Switchable builds are unchanged (still trial-controlled).

  * New OpenSSLStreamAdapter::QaudionHandshakeMeetsPolicy() (BoringSSL only,
    #ifdef OPENSSL_IS_BORINGSSL - this file already forks on that macro for
    every BoringSSL-specific API it uses, and the call site is guarded the
    same way): reads SSL_get_current_cipher()/SSL_CIPHER_get_protocol_id(),
    SSL_get_selected_srtp_profile()->id, SSL_get_group_id() and
    SSL_version() off the just-completed handshake's live SSL*, and checks
    them against the current qaudion::TransportLevel() (srtp cipher at
    level >= 1, DTLS cipher at level >= 2, DTLS version at level >= 3) plus
    qaudion::RequireDtlsPqc() (independent of level, tighten-only)
    unconditionally. Every numeric constant used was verified against the
    pinned BoringSSL headers: TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384 = 0x1302
    (TLS1_3_CK_AES_256_GCM_SHA384 = 0x03001302, tls1.h),
    ECDHE-ECDSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384 = 0xC02C and ECDHE-RSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384 =
    0xC030 (TLS1_CK_..._WITH_AES_256_GCM_SHA384 = 0x0300C02C / 0x0300C030),
    SRTP_AEAD_AES_256_GCM = 0x0008 and SSL_GROUP_X25519_MLKEM768 = 0x11ec
    (both in ssl.h). Logs exactly one ids-only self-report line per
    handshake ("Q-AUDION dtls v=... cipher=... srtp=... group=... level=...
    pqc_req=... ok=..." via absl::StrFormat, already included by this file)
    - numeric protocol/cipher/group ids only, never key material - which is
    both the G3 gate's target string and the operator-facing signal used by
    the app's own DTLS-failure detection (webrtc-plan.md section 3.1: PC
    state FAILED while ICE is CONNECTED/COMPLETED, or this line with
    ok=0).

- rtc_base/ssl_stream_adapter.cc
  (SSLStreamAdapter::GetDefaultEphemeralKeyExchangeCipherGroups):
  in strict builds, X25519MLKEM768 is now the default whenever the
  "WebRTC-EnableDtlsPqc" trial is NOT explicitly "Disabled/" (i.e. on by
  default, no trial needed - both peers are always our own M150 build);
  ".../Disabled/" remains the emergency kill switch. Switchable builds are
  byte-for-byte the original upstream logic (opt-in only via
  ".../Enabled/"). This function alone only controls the *default* list a
  fresh CryptoOptions starts from - see the next file for the other half of
  finding #9.

- api/crypto/crypto_options.cc
  (CryptoOptions::EphemeralKeyExchangeCipherGroups::Update):
  Finding #9 (MEDIUM) root cause, confirmed by reading the real function
  (not just the plan's sketch): Update() computes `default_groups` from
  GetDefaultEphemeralKeyExchangeCipherGroups(field_trials), but then only
  ever ADDS entries from `default_groups` into `enabled_` and re-adds
  everything already in `enabled_` - it never removes anything already
  there. Once X25519MLKEM768 is in `enabled_` (e.g. from the
  EphemeralKeyExchangeCipherGroups default constructor's own call to
  GetDefaultEphemeralKeyExchangeCipherGroups(nullptr), made before any real
  FieldTrialsView exists to check the switch against - the very first
  strict-build change above puts it there unconditionally), calling
  Update() with "WebRTC-EnableDtlsPqc/Disabled/" changed what
  `default_groups` would be, but never touched the group already sitting in
  `enabled_`: the kill switch had no effect, exactly as the review found.
  Fix: an explicit `if (field_trials && field_trials->IsDisabled(
  "WebRTC-EnableDtlsPqc")) { std::erase(enabled_, kX25519_MLKEM768); }`
  right before the add-back loop, using std::erase() (already used the same
  way by this same class's AddFirst(), two functions above) and
  kX25519_MLKEM768 (the class's own portable constant, 0x11ec,
  crypto_options.h:102 - deliberately not the BoringSSL macro, since this
  .cc file does not otherwise depend on BoringSSL's ssl.h being visible).
  This runs regardless of QAUDION_TRANSPORT_STRICT: it is a pure
  correctness fix for the trial's own stated behaviour, gated only by the
  trial value it already reads for `default_groups`.

Depends on Q-Audion's own runtime API (rtc_base/qaudion_tuning.h, written by
P8, applied earlier in the series - see m150/series) for:
- qaudion::TransportLevel() and qaudion::RequireDtlsPqc() - both already
  named in the P8 sketch (plan section 2.3);
- the QAUDION_TRANSPORT_STRICT build-wide #define (also P8).
No new qaudion:: names are introduced by this patch (compare P1's amendment,
which does invent two).

Order: seventh (last) in the series (P1, P2, P3, P8, P4a, P4b, P5, P6, P7 -
see m150/series, owned by the audio/API author). Applied after P6, which
also touches api/crypto/crypto_options.cc (GetSupportedDtlsSrtpCryptoSuites()
and the Srtp::cryptex_policy default) - verified below that this patch's own
crypto_options.cc hunk (against EphemeralKeyExchangeCipherGroups::Update(),
lines 140-172 in the pristine file, well past P6's edits) applies cleanly on
top of P6, unchanged, with no hunk overlap or offset conflict.

